# EXAMPLE term file for pre-publish-scan.py.example — every entry is fictional.
#
# One regex per line. Blank lines and # comments ignored. Case-insensitive.
#
# THIS FILE BELONGS OUTSIDE ANY PUBLISHED TREE. It is committed here only as a
# shape reference. A real term file contains the strings you are protecting, so
# publishing one defeats its own purpose. Keep yours in your private config
# directory, readable only by you.
#
# For GENERATED output, prefer an allowlist over this approach entirely: assert
# that every string in the artifact is one you intended to publish. A denylist
# only catches leaks somebody predicted.

# --- machine and identity ---
/Users/example-user
/home/example-user
\bexample-user\b
first\.last@example\.com

# --- location ---
\bExampleville\b
\b00000\b

# --- private projects ---
\binternal-dashboard\b
\bclient-portal\b

# --- private conventions that are not documented public paths ---
#
# Note what is deliberately ABSENT: the documented install path for a skill or
# plugin. Every public repository that ships one has to print it, so matching it
# produces false positives on legitimate work. The absolute-home-prefix rules
# above already catch the version that actually leaks.
#
# When a pattern false-positives, narrow the pattern and re-run the full test
# suite. Do not exclude the path — that converts an alarm into silence. Write
# the reasoning next to the pattern, or a later edit will simplify it back.
\bprivate-notes-root\b

# --- credential shapes ---
gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{16,}
github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}
sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}
(AKIA|ASIA)[0-9A-Z]{16}
xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{20,}
AIza[0-9A-Za-z_-]{35}
-----BEGIN (RSA |EC |OPENSSH |PGP )?PRIVATE
eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}
